    "case_id": "678",
    "case_summary": "A 12-month-old Caucasian boy presented with convulsive seizures lasting 3-4 seconds, characterized by faltering, smirking with withdrawal of the head and eyes. At birth, the patient had a blond hair strand, polydactyly of the right hand, and cardiac rhabdomyoma. At four months, depigmented spots appeared on the torso. First convulsive seizures noted at 8 months. Psychomotor development was normal for age. No family history of similar conditions.\n\nNo focal neurological symptoms were observed during examinations. Physical features included:- Blond hair strand- Polydactyly of right hand- Depigmented spots on torso\n\n- Imaging Studies:  * Brain CT scan showed:    - Massive neoplasm formation in posterior cranial fossa    - Tubers    - Triventricular hydrocephalus    - Arachnoid cyst of left temporal region      * Brain MRI with contrast revealed:    - Extracranial solid mass in posterior fossa with brain stem compression    - Subcortical tubers in white matter of frontal lobes    - Triventricular hydrocephalus    - Arachnoid cyst of left temporal region      - Genetic Testing:    * Blood sample revealed TSC1 gene mutation (15 exon c.1690 G > T, p.E564*)      - Histopathology:    * Solid lobulated growth pattern    * Large epithelioid cells with clear/eosinophilic cytoplasm    * Moderate to severe cytologic atypia    * 35 mitotic events per high-power field    * Ki67 proliferation index around 15%    * Positive immunostaining for EMA, S100, Pan-Cytokeratin, and Brachyury",
